File: zebra.info,  Node: GNU/Linux configuration,  Next: Configure options,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Installation

GNU/Linux configuration
=======================

   GNU/Linux has very flexible kernel configuration feature.  If you use
GNU/Linux, make it sure kernel configuration is what you want.  Zebra
will run almost kernel configuration but some recommendation exists.

CONFIG_NETLINK
     Kernel/User netlink socket.  This is a brand new feature which
     enable advanced Kernel between Zebra interface (*note Linux
     netlink::.).

CONFIG_RTNETLINK
     Routing messages.  This makes it possible netlink routing message.
     If you specify this option, `zebra' can detect routing information
     updates from the kernel (*note Kernel interface::.).

CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST
     IP: multicasting.  This option should be specified when you uses
     `ripd' or `ospfd' because these protocol use multicast.

   IPv6 supported is added from GNU/Linux kernel version 2.2.  If you
try to use Zebra IPv6 feature on GNU/Linux kernel, please make it sure
below library has been installed.

`inet6-apps'
     `inet6-apps' package includes basic IPv6 related library such as
     `inet_ntop' and `inet_pton'.  Some basic IPv6 programs such as
     `ping', `ftp', `inetd' also included. `inet-apps' can be found at
     `ftp://ftp.inner.net/pub/ipv6/'.

`net-tools'
     `net-tools' provide IPv6 enabled interface and routing utility.  It
     contains `ifconfig', `route', `netstat' and other tools.
     `net-tools' can be found at `ftp://somewhere/'.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Configure options,  Next: VTY port setup,  Prev: GNU/Linux configuration,  Up: Installation

Configure options
=================

   Normally Zebra configure detects host configuration automatically.
For manual configuration there are several configure options.  You can
turn off IPv6 support or disable of making some daemons by configure
options.

`--disable-ipv6'
     Turn off IPv6 related features and daemons.

`--disable-zebra'
     Do not build zebra daemon.

`--disable-bgpd'
     Do not build bgpd.

`--disable-ripd'
     Do not build ripd.

`--disable-ripngd'
     Do not build ripngd.

`--disable-ospfd'
     Do not build ospfd.

`--disable-ospf6d'
     Do not build ospf6d.

`--disable-pthread'
     Do not use pthread features.  Default is disable so you don't need
     specify it.

   You can specify any of above option to the configure script.
Configuration and making program is very simple.  You run `configure'
and then `make' Zebra:

     % ./configure --disable-ipv6
     % make

   will create zebra and other beasts.  Installation is done by:

     % make install

   This will install all of Zebra software under PREFIX/sbin.  Sample
configuration installed under PREFIX/etc.  If you want to change the
installation directory.  Please speicify below option to the configure.

`--prefix=PREFIX'
     Install architecture-independent files in PREFIX [/usr/local].

`--sysconfdir=DIR'
     Read-only sample configuration file in DIR [PREFIX/etc].


File: zebra.info,  Node: VTY port setup,  Prev: Configure options,  Up: Installation

VTY port setup
==============

   Zebra daemon's has each own terminal interface which called VTY.  So
after Zebra beasts installation you have to setup each progam's port
number for connecting to daemons.  Please add below entry to
`/etc/services'.

     zebrasrv      2600/tcp		  # zebra service
     zebra         2601/tcp		  # zebra vty
     ripd          2602/tcp		  # RIPd vty
     ripngd        2603/tcp		  # RIPngd vty
     ospfd         2604/tcp		  # OSPFd vty
     bgpd          2605/tcp		  # BGPd vty

   If you use FreeBSD newer than 2.2.8, above entry is already added to
`/etc/services'.  So there is no need of adding.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Configuration File,  Next: VTY,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Top

Configration File
*****************

   Zebra daemon has it's own configuration file.  For example zebra's
default configuration file name is `zebra.conf'.  Program name plus
`.conf' is default configuration file name. You can specify
configration file by -f or -config-file option to the daemon.

   In configuration file, you can write debug option, vty's password,
routing daemon configuration, logfile name and so on.

* Menu:

* Basic configuration::
* Sample Configuration::


File: zebra.info,  Node: Basic configuration,  Next: Sample Configuration,  Prev: Configuration File,  Up: Configuration File

Basic configuration
===================

`hostname HOSTNAME'
     Set hostname of the router.

`password PASSWORD'
     Set password for vty interface.  If there is no password vty don't
     accept connection.

`enable password PASSWORD'
     Set enable password.

`log TYPE'
     TYPE can be `stdout'.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Sample Configuration,  Prev: Basic configuration,  Up: Configuration File

Sample Configuration
====================

   Below is sample configuration of zebra daemon.

     !
     ! Zebra configration file
     !
     hostname Router
     password zebra
     enable password zebra
     !
     log stdout

   '!' and '#' are comment character.  If the first character of the
word is one of comment character, from the character to the end of line
is ignored as commecnt.

     password zebra!password

   If comment character is not the first character of the word, it's a
normal character. So above '!' doesn't regard as comment and password is
set to 'zebra!password'.


